          <p>
            The membership line is the same for all three machines: $599.88 a year, $1,799.64 over
            three years and $2,999.40 over five. The Tread Flex’s expert assembly was shown as $150
            in one place and $250 in another when we checked in October, which is why we have
            assumed self-assembly; add whichever figure Peloton quotes at checkout if you would
            rather not build a 290 lb machine yourself.
          </p>
          <p>
            One way to make these numbers concrete is cost per workout. Three sessions a week for
            three years is 468 workouts. On the Tread Vision’s three-year total that is about $11.60
            a session, roughly the price of a single drop-in class at many gyms. Five sessions a week
            brings it to about $7. Skipped weeks push it the other way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="line-by-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Every Line of the Bill, Explained</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The machine</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton’s US site showed three treadmills on 9 November 2026. The <strong>Tread Flex</strong>,
            from $2,195, folds and has a 21.5-inch screen, a 59 by 20-inch belt and a 2 HP DC motor by
            Peloton’s figures. The <strong>Tread Vision</strong>, from $3,495, has a cushioned belt of
            the same size, a 3 HP AC motor, a 23.8-inch swivel screen and a movement-tracking camera.
            The <strong>Tread+ Vision</strong>, at $6,695, has a 67-inch slat belt and a 0 to 15%
            incline. All three reach 12.5 mph. The Tread Vision was also on Amazon at the same
            $3,495, sold as a Peloton listing, so Amazon is not a way to pay less for the machine,
            though it can change whose return terms apply.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and assembly</h3>
          <p>
            When we checked Peloton’s site for our main guide on 20 October 2026, delivery was free
            if you assembled the Tread Flex or Tread Vision yourself, expert assembly was listed at
            $150 for the Vision, and the Tread+ Vision carried a required $299 assembly fee. Peloton’s
            pages on 9 November did not repeat those figures, so confirm them in the basket. Peloton
            also notes that extra fees may apply outside the continental US.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The All-Access Membership</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton prices every Tread with the membership “sold separately”. Peloton’s own Amazon
            listing for its Cross Training Tread gave it as $49.99 a month on 9 November 2026, and
            coverage of Peloton’s October 2025 price change gives the same figure, up from $44. The
            membership unlocks the classes, programs and most of the screen’s features for everyone
            in the household. Without it, Peloton says the current Treads offer a basic manual mode,
            which makes a very expensive manual treadmill. Our comparison of <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT and Peloton</a> sets the two big subscription platforms side by side.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Financing</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton offers financing through Affirm from 0% APR with $0 down, subject to approval.
            The 12-month figures on its site were $182.92 a month for the Tread Flex, $291.25 for the
            Tread Vision and $557.92 for the Tread+ Vision, which add up to the cash price. In that
            first year, the membership sits on top: a Tread Vision buyer on the 12-month plan pays
            about $341 a month until the machine is paid off. If you are offered a rate above 0%,
            the total rises, so check the APR before you accept.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Haulaway, returns and the warranty</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Haulaway.</strong> Peloton offers removal of old equipment through Taskrabbit
            at a member rate of $155.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns.</strong> There is a 30-day home trial. If the Tread is not for you,
            Peloton collects it and refunds the order minus a return fee, whose amount the pages we
            read did not state. Ask before ordering.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> Peloton lists a 12-month limited warranty, a three-year
            belt and motor guarantee and a five-year frame guarantee on the Tread Flex and Tread
            Vision, and a five-year belt and motor guarantee on the Tread+ Vision. Anything after
            those periods is a repair bill.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="extras" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Extras People Forget to Budget For</h2>
<p>
            Beyond the machine and the membership, a few costs turn up in most Peloton households.
            We have not priced Peloton’s own accessories, so treat these as a checklist rather than
            a sum.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A mat.</strong> A Tread Vision weighs 316 lb by Peloton’s figure. A
            purpose-made mat protects the floor and reduces noise to the room below. Our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats guide</a> covers sizes for long decks.</li>
            <li><strong>A heart-rate monitor.</strong> Peloton’s Run Analysis asks you to pair a
            compatible heart-rate monitor for full data. A chest strap is the accurate choice; see
            our <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate monitors</a> guide.</li>
            <li><strong>Electrical work.</strong> Peloton’s treadmills draw 12 to 15 amps on a 120-volt
            supply by its figures, and our main guide notes they need a dedicated, grounded circuit.
            If the room does not have one, an electrician belongs in the budget.</li>
            <li><strong>Space and ceiling height.</strong> Peloton’s Amazon listing for the Cross
            Training Tread asks for 78.7 inches of clearance behind the treadmill, 24 inches on the
            other sides and at least an eight-foot ceiling. That is a room decision as much as a
            cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Running electricity.</strong> Small but real; our <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill electricity guide</a> shows how to estimate it.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

